## useState

## Basic Usage

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
const [user, setUser]   = useState<User | null>(null);
const [open, setOpen]   = useState(false);
```

## Functional Updates

When the new state depends on the previous, use the functional form to avoid stale closures:

```tsx
// Unsafe — may read stale count in concurrent mode
setCount(count + 1);

// Safe — always gets the latest value
setCount(prev => prev + 1);
```

## Lazy Initialisation

If the initial value is expensive to compute, pass a function — it runs only on the first render:

```tsx
// Bad — JSON.parse runs on every render
const [data, setData] = useState(JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('data') ?? '{}'));

// Good — runs once
const [data, setData] = useState(() => JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('data') ?? '{}'));
```

## Object State

React does not deep-merge object state — you must spread the previous state manually:

```tsx
const [form, setForm] = useState({ name: '', email: '' });

// Correct
setForm(prev => ({ ...prev, name: 'Aaron' }));

// Wrong — loses email field
setForm({ name: 'Aaron' });
```

## Batching (React 18)

React 18 batches all state updates automatically, even in async callbacks — multiple `setState` calls in the same event only cause one re-render.

